WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.400
Eval gates, shadow and canary deploys.

2
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.830
Production catches everything else.

3
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:10.333
So we need a loop that learns from real use, not just a gate before merge.

4
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:14.933
A gate only stops failures you already know to check for.

5
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:18.800
The feedback loop is what makes tomorrow's gate smarter than today's.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Here is the loop.

7
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.900
Every change first runs the golden set suite before merge.

8
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.467
Then it ships to real traffic, in a canary or a shadow.

9
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:33.700
Online signals come in: thumbs up or down, task completion, escalation rate.

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.633
And those failures become new golden rows, so the loop closes.

11
00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:40.567
What is the difference between the two?

12
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:43.667
A canary serves a small slice of real users.

13
00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:47.600
A shadow runs alongside production, without affecting anyone.

14
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:52.967
Either way, online signals flow back so the golden set keeps learning from production.

15
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:57.833
A real case: a canary of Mission Control RAG version two.

16
00:00:58.133 --> 00:01:00.300
It gets three thumbs down on the same day.

17
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:06.100
All three are multi-hop questions that the router sent to vector search instead of the graph.

18
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.659
Those transcripts become three new golden set rows, with the correct routing labeled.

19
00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:14.567
The next eval run would catch it before canary.

20
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:18.533
The mistake is fixing the incident and moving on.

21
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:23.958
Production incidents that don't become golden set rows are lessons paid for twice.

22
00:01:23.958 --> 00:01:25.333
Make every one a test.

23
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:27.263
Let's recap.

24
00:01:27.263 --> 00:01:30.000
Offline evals catch what you thought to test.

25
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.767
Canary and shadow expose real traffic.

26
00:01:33.067 --> 00:01:35.800
Online signals feed back into the golden set.

27
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.170
So tomorrow's gate is smarter.

28
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:41.000
Next up: why AI reshapes test automation.
